# Verification before completion

## The iron law

**No completion claim without fresh command evidence produced in this turn.**
"Tests pass," "it's fixed," "done," a commit message, or a PR-prep step are all
completion claims. Each one requires you to have actually run the relevant
command (test suite, typecheck, lint, build) in the current turn and read its
real output — not a memory of an earlier run, not an assumption from reading
the diff, and not a subagent's self-report taken at face value.

## Skipped / `0 tests` is not green

A suite that skipped or collected `0 tests` proves nothing about the code —
see [test-execution-integrity.mdc](.cursor/rules/test-execution-integrity.mdc) for the full
`describe.skipIf(!authEnvReady)` mechanics and the strict run command. This
rule generalizes that doctrine beyond the TDD loop: **any** command whose
output shows a skip, an empty collection, or a non-zero exit disqualifies the
completion claim it was meant to support, in `/commit`'s gate re-runs and
everywhere else.

## A subagent's "success" report is a claim, not evidence

When a delegated phase (`tdd-red`/`tdd-green`/`tdd-refactor`, or any other
subagent) reports GREEN, PASS, or "done," that report is the subagent's claim
about its own work — it is not, by itself, the orchestrator's evidence. Before
advancing past that phase or citing it as done:

- **Re-check the diff.** Confirm the files the subagent claims to have changed
  actually changed the way it says.
- **Re-run (or confirm the run of) the command it cites.** Don't advance a
  phase on the strength of a pasted "Result: GREEN ✓" line alone if the actual
  command output isn't visible in the same turn.

This is not distrust of the subagent's competence — it is refusing to let a
completion claim travel further than the evidence that backs it.

## Verify the world, not the self-report

An assertion about the workspace must re-run the command or re-read the file
from disk. A keyword probe on the agent's own previous output lets a cheating
(or merely stale) agent pass. When the claim is that a file was not changed,
assert the untouched bytes are identical to the prior read — not that the
transcript lacks an edit mention.

## A guard is trusted only after it has blocked

A hook or checker is only trusted once the regression it claims to block has
been introduced, observed failing that guard, and reverted. An assertion that
passes against a guard that never blocked anything proves nothing.

## PowerShell examples

```powershell
# Re-verify a unit suite before citing it as green
pnpm test:unit tests/unit/lib/admin/apply-admin-job-board-filters.test.ts

# Re-verify an integration suite (fail-closed: a skip or down DB must fail hard)
pnpm test:integration tests/integration/jobs/accept-offer.integ.test.ts

# Re-verify types and lint before a commit/PR-prep completion claim
pnpm typecheck
pnpm lint
```

## Where this applies

- [`commit.md`](.cursor/commands/commit.md) — before re-running the gates
  (§2), the completion claim about to be issued (PASS) needs this turn's
  fresh command output, not the run's remembered report.
- [`sdd-to-tdd.md`](.cursor/commands/sdd-to-tdd.md) — do not mark a phase
  done on a subagent's assertion alone; the orchestrator's own phase-exit
  check is what actually gates advancing to the next Task call.
- [`tdd-red.md`](.cursor/agents/tdd-red.md) / [`tdd-green.md`](.cursor/agents/tdd-green.md) /
  [`tdd-refactor.md`](.cursor/agents/tdd-refactor.md) — each phase's own
  report is expected to cite the exact command it ran and the exact result
  observed, not a paraphrase.
